rs from around the world who were eager to engage with some of the most challenging questions in cognition, computation, and machine intelligence. In the 1970s and 80s, as the limitations of purely symbolic AI became apparent, MIT researchers were among the first to seriously re-examine neural networks, with pioneers like Terry Sejnowski and Geoffrey Hinton (who spent time at MIT before moving to the University of Toronto) collaborating on models such as the Boltzmann machine, laying groundwork for the deep learning revolution decades later; simultaneously, MIT\u2019s Department of Brain and Cognitive Sciences, co-founded by Minsky and others, fostered a uniquely integrative approach that treated intelligence as a phenomenon to be studied from multiple angles\u2014computational, psychological, neurobiological\u2014and this interdisciplinary ethos remains a hallmark of MIT\u2019s AI research today. The creation of CSAIL in 2003, merging the AI Lab with the Laboratory for Computer Science, further solidified MIT\u2019s commitment to large-scale, collaborative AI research, and since then, CSAIL has produced a staggering array of breakthroughs, from the development of probabilistic programming languages like Church and Stan (with contributors such as Daniel Roy and David Sontag) that enable more flexible and uncertain reasoning, to the creation of novel optimization algorithms that power modern machine learning pipelines, including stochastic gradient descent variants and adaptive methods that underpin the training of large-scale models. In computer vision, MIT researchers like Antonio Torralba and Bill Freeman have made foundational contributions to scene understanding, object recognition, and generative modeling, while in natural language processing, teams led by Regina Barzilay, Tommi Jaakkola, and others have advanced machine reading, question answering, and the application of deep learning to biomedical text mining, with Barzilay\u2019s work on using AI for cancer 